{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[A]I was born in the shadow of a [D]Tennessee
[A]mountain
In a slower and gentler [E]time
In a [D]cabin my grandfather [A]built with his
hands
Out of [A]hickory [E]walnut and [A]pine
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[A]We hunted those hills, my [D]daddy and I[A]
So far from the bustle of [E]town
But I'd [D]soon see a whole way of [A]life start
to fade
As the [A]mines all began to shut [E]down
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[A]Heard saws on the mountain, saw the [D]trucks
rumble [A]by
Filing past like a funeral [E]line
Those [D]big iron trailers were [A]piled up high
With [A]Hickory, [E]Walnut and [A]Pine
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[A]Now the mountain top's flattened, the
[D]hickory's [A]gone
And the streams run black as a [E]curse
My [D]father's been out in the [A]barn more and
more
Since the [A]mining went bad and then [E]worse
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[A]Well I heard his old saw, I [D]heard his old
[A]plane
I heard his cough worsen with [E]time
Now I [D]stand where he lays in a [A]box that he
made
Out of [A]hickory, [E]walnut and [A]pine
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
[A]My children have left, for the [D]big city
[A]jobs
I'll remain, my fate to [E]unwind
And I'll [D]dream of the days when the [A]wind
called my name
Through [A]hickory, [E]walnut and [A]pine
{end_of_verse}
